Topological Defect Spectroscopy
— The Geometric Mechanism of Characteristic Spectral Lines within the TDMU Framework
TDMU Spectroscopy Supplement v1.1

MJP

Abstract

Within the axiomatic system of the Topological Dynamical Membrane Universe (TDMU v2.341), this paper proposes a purely geometric interpretation of characteristic spectra. The core thesis: the atomic nucleus is a composite body formed by the recursive weaving of topological knots. The weaving scars (healed defect-complementarity interfaces) and topological defects (unclosed dangling ports) left behind during this weaving process jointly constitute the geometric origin of spectral activity. Light—the transverse wave perturbation of shared link orientation, δ𝒜—undergoes scattering, capture, and resonant release when traversing these geometrically inhomogeneous regions. The spectral line frequency is jointly determined by the depth of the scar/defect within the recursive weave and the degree of stability of the shared link network at that location; the line width is determined by the smoothness of the scar’s healed surface; the total number of spectral lines is determined by the total number of weaving scars plus topological defects within the depth reachable by the light’s energy. This paper applies this unified mechanism to systems such as hydrogen, iron, diamond, and mercury, and discusses the depth scale in high-energy collisions. All concepts are expressed using native TDMU terminology, without recourse to standard-model concepts such as “energy levels,” “transitions,” or “electron orbits.”

1. The Geometric Origin of Spectral Activity

1.1 Two Types of Geometrically Inhomogeneous Regions

According to TDMU Postulate 8 (Recursive Growth), the atomic nucleus is a giant stable topological knot formed by the recursive, stepwise weaving of sub-topological knots such as primitive bricks and electron bricks via defect-complementarity weaving. During this weaving process, two types of geometrically inhomogeneous regions emerge:

Definition 1.1: Weaving Scar
A region of the shared link network, formed on the meshing interface when sub-topological knots combine through defect-complementarity weaving, that is healed but geometrically non-ideal.
Characteristics of a weaving scar:

The link saturation has reached a local maximum (the weave is closed; it is no longer a “hole”).

Smoothness is inferior to that of an ideal weave surface—the shared link orientation tensor 𝒜_ij on the sutured surface deviates from a uniform arrangement, and residual topological stress exists (suture line stress concentration factor ℐ > 0).

The local stiffness κ_scar differs from that of the surrounding ideal weave region κ_bulk, constituting an impedance mismatch interface.
A weaving scar is a structural imprint of the recursive weaving history inside the topological knot.

Definition 1.2: Topological Defect
An unsaturated dangling port left on the surface of a topological knot where the weave cannot achieve absolutely perfect closure (TDMU Definition 7.2). A topological defect is an active site for nuclear reactions (the trigger position for Pathway I) and simultaneously an active site for spectral absorption.

Distinction between the two types of inhomogeneous regions:

Weaving Scar	Topological Defect
Location	Inside the weave body	Outermost surface of the weave body
Link State	Saturated and closed	Unsaturated (dangling ports exist)
Smoothness	Non-ideally smooth (healing traces exist)	Not applicable (it is an open boundary)
Spectral Role	Impedance mismatch → scattering and resonance	Absorption (breaking the local constraint of the dangling link)
Nuclear Reaction Role	None (already saturated)	Present (trigger site for Pathway I)
1.2 Linkage Zone: A Collective Term for Both Types of Inhomogeneous Regions

Definition 1.3: Linkage Zone
A general term encompassing both weaving scars and topological defects. A linkage zone is a localized region within the weave body where the shared link network exhibits inhomogeneity—whether a healed scar or an unclosed defect, their common feature is that the local link state differs detectably from the surrounding ideal weave region.

A linkage zone possesses three geometric parameters that directly determine spectral characteristics:

Parameter	Definition	Spectral Correspondence
Depth	The spatial position of the linkage zone within the recursive weave body (measured in node steps from the nuclear surface)	Primary determinant of resonance/absorption frequency
Degree of Stability	The average link saturation 𝒮 of the shared links within the linkage zone (high for saturated scars, low for unsaturated defects)	Secondary determinant of resonance/absorption frequency
Smoothness	The geometric deviation of the scar’s healed surface from the ideal weave surface; the boundary irregularity of a defect	Spectral line width
Proposition 1 (Spectral Line Frequency): Spectral line frequency ω = f (Depth, Degree of Stability). Greater depth and higher degree of stability (link saturation 𝒮) → greater local stiffness → higher resonance frequency.

Proposition 2 (Spectral Line Width): Spectral line width Γ = g (Smoothness). Lower smoothness (rougher healed surface, more irregular defect boundary) → greater rate of excited-state energy dissipation → broader line width.

Proposition 3 (Total Number of Spectral Lines): Total number of spectral lines = within the depth range reachable by the light’s energy, the total number of weaving scars (contributing resonant lines) + the total number of topological defects (contributing absorption lines).

1.3 Interaction Mechanism between Light and Linkage Zones

In TDMU, light is defined as the transverse wave perturbation of shared link orientation, δ𝒜 (TDMU Definitions 9.5, 6.1.1).

When δ𝒜 propagates through the shared link network and encounters a linkage zone:

Impedance Mismatch: The local stiffness of the linkage zone κ_linkage differs from the surrounding κ_bulk → a portion of the perturbation is reflected (scattering).

Resonant Capture: If the linkage zone (scar) forms an effective resonant cavity (with boundary conditions determined by the suture line geometry), δ𝒜 of a specific frequency is captured and oscillates at the cavity’s natural frequency.

Resonant Release: The captured energy is released through tunneling or boundary reconnection dissipation → emission spectral line.

Absorption: If the linkage zone is a topological defect (unsaturated dangling port), the energy of the incident δ𝒜 is absorbed to break the local constraint of that port → absorption spectral line.

Herein lies the geometric mechanism for both absorption and emission spectra.

2. Hydrogen: The Weave Body with the Lowest Recursive Order

2.1 Geometric Structure

The hydrogen nucleus is the lowest-order stable topological knot on the recursive growth tree in the TDMU framework—formed by the minimum number of underlying topological knots (primitive bricks) through the fewest steps of defect-complementarity weaving.

Key Clarification: “Lowest recursive order” does not equate to “having only one layer of weaving.” Any recursive weaving produces a multi-layered structure—even the hydrogen nucleus contains splicing interfaces between primitive bricks (weaving scars) inside and unsaturated dangling ports (topological defects) on its surface. These linkage zones are distributed across various depths from the core center to the surface.

Geometric features of the hydrogen nucleus:

Minimal number of recursive layers → shortest weaving history.

Extremely few total linkage zones—yet they exist at every depth (deep: brick-splicing scars; intermediate: interlayer interface scars; surface: weave-closure scars and surface defects).

Fewest weaving layers → minimal cumulative weaving error → extremely high smoothness for all linkage zones.

2.2 Spectral Facts

The hydrogen atomic spectrum consists of several series (Lyman, Balmer, Paschen, etc.), with frequencies satisfying the Rydberg formula. The number of spectral lines is relatively small, and the line widths are extremely narrow.

Experimental Benchmarks:

Lyman series (UV): wavelengths 91–122 nm

Balmer series (visible): wavelengths 364–656 nm

Paschen series (IR): wavelengths 820–1875 nm

Typical natural line width: Δλ ~ 10⁻⁵ nm (corresponding lifetime ~ 10⁻⁸ s)

2.3 TDMU Interpretation

Small number of spectral lines: The total number of linkage zones is extremely small. However, linkage zones exist at every depth layer, so the hydrogen spectrum still presents multiple series spanning UV to IR.

Extremely narrow line widths: Fewest weaving layers → minimal cumulative weaving error → extremely high smoothness for all scars → extremely slow excited-state energy dissipation → very sharp lines.

Integer-ratio relationship of frequencies: Harmonic structure of resonance modes in a finite-scale linkage zone—each linkage zone, acting as a resonant cavity, supports a fundamental frequency and a series of integer-multiple overtones. This is a universal mathematical property of isolated, finite-boundary resonant structures and is not unique to quantum mechanics.

Different series correspond to different depths: The Lyman series corresponds to deep linkage zone resonance (high frequency); the Balmer series corresponds to intermediate-depth linkage zone resonance; the Paschen series corresponds to shallow linkage zone resonance. The differences in wavelength ranges reflect the systematic change in linkage zone depth: deep zones have higher local stiffness → higher resonance frequency → shorter wavelengths (UV); shallow zones have lower local stiffness → lower resonance frequency → longer wavelengths (IR).

Consistency Check with Experiment:
The 1/n² form of the Rydberg formula is interpreted in this framework as the harmonic structure of the linkage zone resonant cavity. In the standard model, *n* is the principal quantum number; here, *n* corresponds to the harmonic order of the resonance mode. The two are mathematically equivalent but differ in physical ontology. This framework cannot derive the precise value of the Rydberg constant from first principles (this would require knowing the specific dimensions and local stiffness constants of the linkage zone resonant cavities); it remains a free parameter to be determined by experiment.

Potential Inconsistency with Experiment:
The Lyman and Balmer series of hydrogen possess strict selection rules (e.g., Δl = ±1) that determine which frequency lines can appear. This framework currently cannot derive an equivalent selection rule from its geometric mechanism. Preliminary speculation: Selection rules may originate from the matching condition between the polarization direction of the incident light δ𝒜 and the geometric symmetry of the resonant cavity—only modes whose symmetry axis matches the polarization can be effectively excited. This speculation awaits further justification.

Conclusion: The simple spectrum of hydrogen is a direct consequence of being “the weave body with the lowest recursive order, possessing an extremely small total number of linkage zones, yet with zones distributed at all depths.”

3. Iron: The Rich Scar System of Deep Recursive Weaving

3.1 Geometric Structure

The iron nucleus (predominantly Fe-56) is a giant stable topological knot formed by the multi-level recursive weaving of 26 chiral topological clusters (carrying unit positive charge) and other neutral topological clusters. Its recursive volume far exceeds that of the hydrogen nucleus:

Many recursive layers—the weaving history of 26 chiral clusters involves a large number of independent steps.

Numerous splicing boundaries at each layer—defect-complementarity weaving between chiral clusters leaves scars at every level.

Linkage zones are distributed across all depths from surface to core, with the number at each depth far exceeding that of hydrogen.

The distribution of linkage zones across depths is relatively uniform—a natural consequence of multi-level weaving, where each weaving stage contributes scars at corresponding depths.

3.2 Spectral Facts

The visible spectrum of iron is exceptionally line-rich (tens of thousands of recorded lines in the visible band alone), covering an extremely broad band from infrared to hard X-rays. The iron-56 nucleus is one of the most stable nuclides in the universe (highest binding energy per nucleon).

Experimental Benchmarks:

The spectral line database for neutral iron atoms (Fe I) records over 20,000 lines.

Spectral coverage: from near-infrared (~1 μm) to hard X-rays (~0.01 nm, K-shell characteristic energy ~6.4 keV).

The average binding energy per nucleon for Fe-56 is ~8.79 MeV, the highest of all nuclides.

Neighboring nuclides (Fe-54, Fe-57, Fe-58) possess nearly identical chemical properties but significantly different nuclear properties (e.g., neutron capture cross-sections).

3.3 TDMU Interpretation

Extremely rich spectrum: The total number of weaving scars and topological defects in the iron nucleus is vast. Each scar corresponds to a set of resonant modes, and each defect corresponds to an absorption feature. The tens of thousands of lines are the superposition of independent contributions from a multitude of linkage zones.

Broad band coverage: Linkage zones are distributed relatively uniformly across all depths—from surface to core, each depth has a sufficient number of linkage zones to respond to light of the corresponding energy scale. Surface scars produce low-frequency lines (IR-visible), while deep scars produce high-frequency lines (X-rays). This aligns with the multi-level weaving history of the iron nucleus: the recursive weaving of 26 chiral clusters is a multi-stage process, with scars accumulating uniformly at every depth level.

Extreme nuclear stability: Although numerous surface and mid-level linkage zones exist (producing the rich spectrum), the deep linkage zones of the iron nucleus have highly ordered shared link arrangements, with core link saturation 𝒮_core approaching the maximum and suture line stress concentration factor ℐ extremely low. This is the geometric root of the iron nucleus possessing the highest E_comp (binding energy per nucleon).

No contradiction: Linkage zones at different depths are independently probed by light of different energy scales. Low-energy light (visible) only reaches surface linkage zones; high-energy light (X-rays) is needed to probe deep linkage zones. The richness of the surface spectrum and the stability of the deep structure are carried by completely independent geometric degrees of freedom and do not conflict.

Consistency Check with Experiment:

The highest binding energy per nucleon for Fe-56 is consistent with this framework’s deduction of “deep linkage zones 𝒮_core→1, ℐ→0.”

The far greater number of spectral lines for iron compared to light nuclei (H, He) is consistent with the deduction of “large recursive volume → many scars.”

The characteristic X-ray lines of iron (Kα ~6.4 keV, Kβ ~7.1 keV) correspond to deep linkage zone resonance, consistent with the framework’s depth-frequency mapping.

Conclusion: The extreme complexity of the iron spectrum is a direct result of the “iron nucleus possessing a vast number of weaving scars distributed across all depths.” The extreme stability of the iron nucleus is a result of “near-perfect deep weaving.” The two are not contradictory.

4. Diamond: Saturation Pairing of All Linkage Zones

4.1 Geometric Structure

In a diamond crystal, the topological defects on the surface of each carbon nucleus undergo saturation pairing with the corresponding defects of adjacent carbon nuclei through Pathway I (defect-complementarity weaving). The entire crystal forms a macroscopic, rigid-body shared link network with no isolated linkage zones.

Key geometric features:

All surface defects of carbon nuclei are saturation-paired → no isolated unsaturated defects.

Weaving scars still exist (traces of recursive weaving inside the carbon nucleus), but the defect-complementarity weaving causes the scars of adjacent nuclei to interlock, forming a globally rigid link network spanning the whole crystal.

Link lengths are highly uniform throughout the crystal (a geometric necessity of the periodic lattice structure).

4.2 Spectral Facts

Diamond exhibits no characteristic atomic spectral lines. It shows only a single collective lattice vibration peak (first-order Raman peak at 1332 cm⁻¹) with extremely narrow width (~2 cm⁻¹). The absorption edge is steep (indirect band gap ~5.5 eV).

Experimental Benchmarks:

First-order Raman peak: 1332 cm⁻¹ (room temperature), full width at half maximum ~1.5–2 cm⁻¹.

UV absorption edge: ~225 nm (~5.5 eV), corresponding to the indirect band gap.

Transparent in the visible to near-infrared region, with no detectable atomic absorption or emission lines.

4.3 TDMU Interpretation

No characteristic atomic spectral lines: All topological defects have been saturation-paired; no isolated dangling ports exist for light absorption. Although weaving scars exist inside the carbon nuclei, their independent resonance is suppressed by the rigid global shared link network—scars cannot operate as independent resonant cavities because their boundary conditions are locked by the rigid coupling of adjacent scars.

Single lattice vibration peak: The global shared link network of the entire crystal possesses only one fundamental collective vibration mode—all links stretching and contracting in phase. 1332 cm⁻¹ is the characteristic frequency of this collective mode.

Extremely sharp peak: Link lengths are highly consistent throughout the crystal (periodic lattice), resulting in extremely low energy dissipation for the collective vibration mode.

Steep absorption edge: 5.5 eV corresponds to the minimum energy threshold required to break a link within the globally rigid link network. Below this energy, no dissipative channel is available.

Consistency Check with Experiment:

The Raman peak frequency and width of diamond are qualitatively consistent with this framework’s expectation of “a single collective mode of a globally rigid network.”

The absence of characteristic atomic spectral lines in diamond is consistent with the deduction of “no isolated defects = no independent atomic spectral lines.”

The existence and steepness of the absorption edge are consistent with the deduction of “a collective energy threshold of the globally rigid network.” However, the specific value of 5.5 eV is a free parameter that this framework cannot derive from first principles.

Conclusion: Diamond is spectroscopically “silent”—all surface defects are saturation-paired with adjacent carbon nuclei, and the independent resonance of internal scars is suppressed by the globally rigid network. Light can only perceive the collective vibration of the crystal.

5. Mercury: Collective Freezing of Linkage Zones

5.1 Geometric Structure

Mercury (Hg) is liquid at room temperature. The relative positions of atomic nuclei constantly change, and the linkage zones of each nucleus are in a thermally activated state—vibrating independently with no global phase correlation.

When the temperature drops below Tc (~4.2 K), mercury enters the superconducting state. In the TDMU framework (Definition 9.3), the superconducting state corresponds to:

D_ij Θ = 0, for all nearest-neighbor node pairs ⟨i, j⟩

That is, all linkage zones achieve global phase synchronization—the helical motions of all shared links are perfectly synchronous.

5.2 Spectral Facts

Normal state (T > Tc): Far-infrared reflectivity is flat, around 90%.

Superconducting state (T ≪ Tc): Far-infrared reflectivity jumps abruptly to nearly 100% at a characteristic frequency, with a weak absorption dip at the threshold frequency.

Experimental Benchmarks: The far-infrared reflectivity of superconducting mercury jumps from ~90% to >99.9% for frequencies below about 10¹¹ Hz; an absorption threshold appears at roughly 3.5k_BT_c (~3×10¹¹ Hz, corresponding to an energy gap of ~1.2 meV).

5.3 TDMU Interpretation

Normal state: The linkage zones of different nuclei vibrate independently (thermally activated). Incident light δ𝒜 may encounter impedance mismatch at each linkage zone → partial scattering, partial absorption and dissipation → moderate reflectivity (~90%), no sharp spectral features.

Superconducting state: All linkage zones enter a globally phase-locked state (D_ij Θ = 0, ∀⟨i, j⟩). In this state, incident light δ𝒜 with frequency below a certain threshold cannot excite any asynchronous perturbation in the shared link network—because the motion of all links is locked, δ𝒜 must break this global lock to be absorbed. Low-frequency light cannot break the lock → it is completely reflected (rigid reflection, reflectivity approaching 100%).

Threshold frequency: Corresponds to the minimum energy required to break the D_ij Θ = 0 global lock. At this threshold, the incident light can just excite linkage zones out of the synchronous state, creating an absorption dip. The threshold frequency is phenomenologically equivalent to the superconducting energy gap Δ.

Consistency Check with Experiment:

The jump in reflectivity from ~90% to >99.9% in the superconducting state is consistent with this framework’s deduction of “rigid reflection from global locking.”

The existence of a threshold frequency and its energy scale (~3.5k_BT_c) is qualitatively consistent with the framework’s description of “minimum energy required to break synchronization.” However, the specific numerical value (3.5k_BT_c) cannot be derived from first principles and is a free parameter.

The flat reflectivity in the normal state is consistent with the deduction of “thermally activated independent scattering.”

Conclusion: The superconducting phase transition is a collective synchronization transition of all linkage zones. In the normal state, zones scatter light independently. In the superconducting state, low-frequency light encounters a fully synchronized, rigid shared link network, cannot be absorbed, and is totally reflected.

6. Chemical Shift and Molecular Spectra

6.1 Chemical Shift

When atoms bond to form molecules, the topological defects on the surfaces of two nuclei couple through Pathway I (defect-complementarity weaving), forming new shared link channels (chemical bonds).

This coupling modifies the boundary conditions of the participating linkage zones:

Compressed (link shortened): The shared link network is tightened → local stiffness κ increases → the resonance frequency of the scar rises → spectral line blue shift.

Stretched (link elongated): The shared link network is relaxed → local stiffness κ decreases → the resonance frequency of the scar drops → spectral line red shift.

Consistency with Experiment:

Spectral frequency shifts caused by changes in chemical environment (chemical shifts) are widely observed experimentally. For example, the resonance frequency of hydrogen in different chemical environments (H₂O vs. CH₄ vs. HF) shows small but measurable differences.

This framework interprets this phenomenon as: the geometric constraints of different chemical bonds (link length, link angle) alter the effective stiffness of the participating scars.

6.2 New Features in Molecular Spectra

After molecule formation, new spectral features appear beyond the linkage zone resonances of the original nuclei:

Resonance modes of the molecular bond itself: The new linkage zone formed by the defect-complementarity weaving of surface defects from two (or more) nuclei possesses its own independent resonant frequencies. These frequencies typically fall in the infrared region (lower frequency, because the scale of the molecular bond linkage zone is larger than that of intra-nuclear linkage zones).

Hierarchical coupling modes: The vibration of the molecular bond (change in linkage zone scale) couples through the shared link network to the resonant frequencies of intra-nuclear scars, producing frequency modulation → leading to the appearance of main peaks and sideband structures.

This is the geometric explanation for the emergence of new spectral lines (characteristic IR peaks) and fine structures (vibronic coupling) in molecular spectra. In the TDMU framework, this is not “electronic energy level transitions” but rather “coupled resonance of linkage zones across different weaving levels.”

7. High-Energy Collisions: The Depth Scale

The energy of an incident topological knot (or equivalently, the energy of incident light/particle) determines the weaving depth it can penetrate:

Energy Scale	Probeable Depth of Linkage Zones	Corresponding Phenomena
eV range	Surface topological defects and near-surface scars	Chemical bond breaking and rearrangement
keV–MeV	Mid-level weaving scars	Characteristic X-rays (inner-layer scar resonance)
MeV–GeV	Deep weaving scars and suture lines	Nuclear fission (tearing along stress-concentrated suture lines)
TeV and above	Recursive structures of sub-topological knots within the nucleus	Deep inelastic scattering (jets corresponding to sub-knot disassembly)
Corollary 7.1 (Depth Selectivity of Fission Products):

If the energy of the incident topological knot can be precisely controlled to act only on a weaving scar at a specific depth, the compound nucleus will preferentially tear along the suture line where that scar is located. In this limit, the mass distribution of fission products will tend toward determinism rather than a statistical distribution.

Current Experimental Observation: When low-energy neutrons induce ²³⁵U fission, the product mass distribution exhibits a double-humped statistical distribution. This framework interprets this as: although the incident neutron energy is relatively definite (thermal neutron ~0.025 eV), the ²³⁵U nucleus has ℐ > 0 (core vacancies, see TDMU Corollary 8.2), meaning multiple stress-concentrated suture lines exist. Fission can occur along any suture line with a relatively high triggering probability, resulting in the statistical nature of the product distribution.

Testable Implication: If an experiment could be designed to selectively excite a scar at a specific depth using mono-energetic gamma rays, the mass distribution of fission products should narrow and deviate from the conventional double-humped distribution, trending toward specific mass numbers. This corollary is potentially falsifiable.

