9. Conclusion

The atomic nucleus is a recursively woven ball, pasted layer by layer. During the weaving process, the weaving scars left by the healing of defect-complementarity engagements and the topological defects left unclosed on the surface are the geometric sources of spectral activity.

Spectral line frequency = f (Linkage zone depth × Degree of stability). Deep scars → high local stiffness → high frequency.

Spectral line width = g (Scar smoothness). Low smoothness (rough healed surface) → fast excited-state dissipation → broad line width.

Total number of spectral lines = within the depth accessible to the light’s energy, the total number of scars + total number of defects. Scars contribute resonant emission/absorption; defects contribute absorption.

Hydrogen: Extremely few linkage zones but distributed at all depths → few lines yet covering multiple bands.

Iron: Extremely numerous linkage zones, uniformly distributed → exceptionally rich lines covering a wide band.

Diamond: All surface defects saturation-paired with adjacent nuclei → spectroscopically silent.

Superconducting mercury: All linkage zones globally synchronized → low-frequency light is rigidly reflected.

This paper does not use standard-model concepts such as “energy levels,” “transitions,” “electron orbits,” or “photons.” All explanations are based on native terminology within the TDMU axiomatic system: weaving scars, topological defects, shared links, orientation transverse wave δ𝒜, link saturation 𝒮, smoothness, and depth.

Appendix: The Status of the Uncertainty Principle in TDMU

The uncertainty relation in standard quantum mechanics, Δx·Δp ≥ ℏ/2, is equivalently expressed in TDMU as:

The product of the spatial localization precision of a linkage zone (measured in node steps) and the precision of the reconnection-event count history of that zone (measured in reconnection events) has a lower bound determined by the resolution limit of the discrete weave network.

This relation is not an a priori postulate of this framework but an emergent statistical property of the discrete combinatorial network in the continuum limit. Its deep reason lies in the fact that each “measurement” (local reconnection probe) of a linkage zone’s position inevitably alters the link state of that region, thereby affecting the precise count of its reconnection history. ℏ corresponds in this framework to the geometric constant of this “minimum disturbance-response product.”

