Topological Defect	An unclosed dangling port on the surface of a weave body. Link-unsaturated. Contributes absorption spectral lines and serves as a site for nuclear reactions (Pathway I).
Linkage Zone	Collective term for weaving scars and topological defects. A localized region where inhomogeneity appears in the shared link network.
Depth	The spatial position of a linkage zone within the recursive weave body, measured in node steps from the nuclear surface.
Degree of Stability	The average link saturation 𝒮 of the shared links within a linkage zone. Higher saturation implies greater local stiffness.
Smoothness	The geometric deviation of a scar’s healed surface from the ideal weave surface; the boundary irregularity of a defect.
Shared Link	The multiple connection pathways established between topological knots via intermediate loops on the membrane.
δ𝒜	The dynamic transverse wave perturbation of shared link orientation, i.e., light.
𝒮	Link saturation, the proportion of occupied links among all possible connection paths.
ℐ	Suture line stress concentration factor, characterizing the geometric non-ideality of a scar’s healed surface.
D_ij Θ	Combinatorial covariant derivative, characterizing the degree of local synchronization of the shared link phase field.


